{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>With Your counsel, You will take me</b> — The word לקיחה (taking) without an object means the joining of the soul of the righteous with the supernal beings, which are not body and do not die forever. Therefore concerning Enoch: ויתהלך חנוך את האלהים (Genesis 5:24) — he accustomed his soul to walk with the angels until HaShem took him. And so this poet asks: help me, guide me, and accustom me to Your counsel, and after You do me this honor, <b>then You will take me</b>; and similarly: כי יקחני סלה (Psalms 49:16)." "Or the interpretation of ואחר (and after) — and afterward You will take me, and I [will be] glory, like one of the angels."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 73, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:34:21.110655500Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}